'''4†.''' To put away or expel; banish; renounce the authority of; dethrone; degrade.
{{smaller block|Scaliger would needs turn down Homer, and ''abdicate'' him after the possession of three thousand years.
{{right|''Dryden'', Pref. to Third Misc.}}}}
{{smaller|='''Syn. 1.''' To resign, renounce, give up, quit, vacate, relinquish, lay down, abandon, desert. (See list under ''abandon'', ''v.'')}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' To renounce or give up something; abandon some claim; relinquish a right, power, or trust.
{{smaller block|He cannot ''abdicate'' for his children, otherwise than by his own consent in form to a bill from the two houses.
{{right|''Swift'', Sent. of Ch. of Eng. Man.}}}}
{{smaller block|Don John is represented . . . to have voluntarily restored the throne to his father, who had once ''abdicated'' in his favor.
{{right|''Ticknor'', Span. Lit., II. 221.}}}}

'''abdicated''' (ab′di-kā-ted), ''p. a.'' Self-deposed; in the state of one who has renounced or given up a right, etc.: as, “the ''abdicated'' Emperor of Austria,” ''Howells'', Venetian Life, xxi.

'''abdication''' (ab-di-kā′shọn), ''n.'' [< L. ''abdicatio''(''n''-), < ''abdicare'': see ''abdicate''.] The act of abdicating; the giving up of an office, power or authority, right or trust, etc.; renunciation; especially, the laying down of a sovereignty hitherto inherent in the person or in the blood.
{{smaller block|The consequences drawn from these facts [were] that they amounted to an ''abdication'' of the government, which ''abdication'' did not only affect the person of the king himself, but also of all his heirs, and rendered the throne absolutely and completely vacant. ''Blackstone'', Com., I. iii.}}
{{smaller block|Each new mind we approach seems to require an ''abdication'' of all our present and past possessions.}}

